  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 201 AM EDT Mon Jul 27 2026\n\nToday-Tonight...Troughiness off of the eastern US sea board is\nforecast today, as strong ridging persists over the central\nportion of the country. Locally, SW flow prevails, increasing into\nthe afternoon, but remaining near to under 10 mph overall.\nRegardless, this is expected to be enough to limit the inland\nextent of the east coast sea breeze today. Thus, there will be little\nrelief from hot and humid temperatures, as highs rise into the\n90s. Heat indices up to 109 degrees are forecast from Orange and\nnorthern Brevard Counties northward, where a Heat Advisory has\nbeen issued, but heat indices 102-107+ are forecast area-wide. When\npossible, reschedule strenuous activities to early morning or \nevening today.\n\nA limiting factor to the heat will be coverage of showers and\nstorms. CAMs have been consistent in developing an early cluster\nof showers and storms along the Nature Coast near sunrise, which \nthen moves SW through central Florida into the early afternoon.\nThis activity will likely drive the heat chances today, though the\nhighest coverage is forecast to be along the western half of the\npeninsula with this first round. The thinking is that this\ncoverage will be widely scattered enough and/or there will be a\nlong enough gap in between rounds of convection to allow\ntemperatures to recover and reach Heat Advisory criteria in the\nadvisory area. Regardless, additional showers and storms are then\nforecast to develop in the mid to late afternoon, with PoPs\ngenerally 40-60% overall today. Storms will be capable of \nproducing frequent lightning strikes, wind gusts up to 50 mph, and\nheavy downpours that may lead to localized flooding. Activity \nwill move offshore and diminish through the evening and overnight \nhours.\n\nTuesday-Wednesday...Low pressure drops through the Great Lakes\nmid-week, flattening the upper level Plains ridge axis over the \nFlorida peninsula. Meanwhile, the Atlantic ridge axis remains \nsouth of east central Florida. The pressure gradient tightens\nbetween these features through the period, increasing SW flow from\naround 10 mph Tuesday afternoon to 15 mph, with gusts 20-25 mph,\non Wednesday afternoon. Ample moisture (PWATs 2-2.15\") and the\npinned east coast sea breeze will maintain PoPs 40-60% Tuesday,\nbefore increased convergence aloft keeps PoPs 30-40% Wednesday. A\nfew strong storms will remain possible, with drier air aloft\nleading to DCAPE values near 1000 J/kg. Other storm hazards will\ninclude lightning strikes and locally heavy downpours, which could\nproduce some localized flooding. W steering flow will see \nactivity push offshore in the afternoon and evening hours, with\ndrier conditions overnight.\n\nHot temperatures in the 90s will continue through mid-week. While\nany showers or storms will be a limiting factor to heat, near to\neven slightly below normal PoPs will still allow for heat indices\nto exceed 100 degrees. Will continue to monitor the need for\nadditional Heat Advisories for at least portions of the area. The\nhottest day this week looks to be Wednesday. Continue to practice\nheat safety, regardless of whether a Heat Advisory is issued.\n\nThursday-Sunday...The Atlantic ridge axis remains across the\nStraits of Florida Thursday, as a low pressure system moves\noffshore from the Northeast. Aloft, a mid/upper level trough digs\nthrough the Southeast and into Florida, despite the associated\ncold front stalling near to just north of the local area. Upper \nlevel support and PWATs near 2\" will lead to higher coverage of \nshowers and storms to end the week. PoPs 50-70% across the area \nwill linger into Saturday, as continued breezy SW flow pins the \neast coast sea breeze. Will see a break from the hotter heat \nindices due to higher coverage of clouds and precipitation. \nHowever, highs are still forecast to reach the lower 90s each \nafternoon. Coverage of showers and storms returns to nearer to \nnormal (40-60%) Sunday into early next week, as ridging develops \naloft.",
